# API Layer Rules (`packages/api-client` — tRPC)

This package is the **composition root**: the only place allowed to import
from both `packages/domain` and `packages/database` in the same file. Routes
and pages never call domain or database packages directly — everything
flows through here.

## Every procedure

- Has a Zod input schema imported from `packages/types` — never an inline
  ad-hoc shape, never a duplicate redefinition of a shape that already
  exists there.
- Re-derives the caller's role/identity from the server-side session
  (`ctx.session`), never trusts a role or userId passed in the input
  payload, even if the client "shouldn't" be able to fake it.
- Returns typed errors via `TRPCError` with an appropriate `code`
  (`UNAUTHORIZED`, `FORBIDDEN`, `BAD_REQUEST`, `NOT_FOUND`) — not a generic
  thrown `Error`, so the client can branch on it meaningfully.

## Composing domain + database

- Fetch the data the domain function needs, call the **pure** domain
  function, then persist the result. Don't reimplement tax/escrow/allocation
  logic inline here "just this once" — if the existing domain function
  doesn't fit, fix the domain function's signature.
- Any domain call that returns a `LedgerEvent` must have that event
  persisted in the same transaction as the state change it describes —
  use a Prisma `$transaction` so the mutation and its audit trail can't
  diverge if one write fails.

## Mutations that touch money or documents

- Booking, escrow transition, and certificate-issuance procedures must be
  idempotent where plausible (e.g., a duplicate webhook retry for the same
  escrow event should not double-charge or double-issue) — check existing
  state before applying a transition, not just before insert.

## Router organization

- One router file per domain area (`booking.ts`, `society.ts`, `dealer.ts`,
  `verification.ts`), merged into a root router — don't grow a single file
  into a catch-all.
